WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.300
Welcome back to Building
HR-Driven Identity.

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.337
This is Chapter 5: certificate-based
authentication checks.

3
00:00:07.337 --> 00:00:10.447
A revoked card is only
revoked if Entra can

4
00:00:10.447 --> 00:00:14.000
download the list that says
so, in time, every time.

5
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.338
So this chapter checks the configuration
behind card sign-in as code.

6
00:00:18.338 --> 00:00:23.897
It starts from Learn's certificate revocation
list page and the Graph resources that

7
00:00:23.897 --> 00:00:27.342
hold the trust store and the
X.509 certificate method,

8
00:00:27.342 --> 00:00:32.275
and adds what they leave to you: a
measurement of every CRL, and thirteen rules.

9
00:00:32.275 --> 00:00:37.640
We'll see why, what the code does,
who runs it, two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:42.977
Why does this gate need code?

11
00:00:42.977 --> 00:00:46.573
The guide's Gate 5 ties the
card's lifecycle to the person's:

12
00:00:46.573 --> 00:00:50.627
the card is issued from a joiner
record and revoked from a leaver.

13
00:00:50.627 --> 00:00:54.477
But revocation only reaches
sign-in through the CRL,

14
00:00:54.477 --> 00:00:57.252
and a CRL grows with every revocation.

15
00:00:57.252 --> 00:01:02.052
The day it crosses the limit is the day
sign-in fails for every card from that CA.

16
00:01:02.052 --> 00:01:03.877
So the check runs every day.

17
00:01:03.877 --> 00:01:07.026
Per-user binding coverage is
the validation kit's job;

18
00:01:07.026 --> 00:01:11.470
this chapter checks the tenant's
settings, which that kit doesn't read.

19
00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.220
Which cloud you're in changes the answer.

20
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:23.226
For interactive sign-in, a CRL must stay
under twenty megabytes in public Entra ID,

21
00:01:23.226 --> 00:01:29.232
which includes GCC, and forty-five in Azure
US Government, which includes GCC High.

22
00:01:29.232 --> 00:01:32.082
Either way it must download
within ten seconds.

23
00:01:32.082 --> 00:01:35.282
So the expected-configuration
file holds both sets,

24
00:01:35.282 --> 00:01:38.183
and the check takes a
cloud name with no default.

25
00:01:38.183 --> 00:01:40.621
Learn doesn't say which megabyte it means,

26
00:01:40.621 --> 00:01:44.850
so the configuration reads the
decimal one, the stricter reading.

27
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.712
Learn's CRL page is Microsoft's
procedure and its limits.

28
00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:57.475
Without a configured CRL, Entra
doesn't check revocation for that CA.

29
00:01:57.475 --> 00:02:02.988
There's one endpoint per CA, and
HTTP is recommended over HTTPS.

30
00:02:02.988 --> 00:02:08.043
A configured CRL that can't be downloaded
fails sign-in for that CA's certificates,

31
00:02:08.043 --> 00:02:12.946
so Learn asks for highly available
endpoints and for CRL validation enforced,

32
00:02:12.946 --> 00:02:15.813
with exemptions only
while troubleshooting.

33
00:02:15.813 --> 00:02:20.896
Graph holds the configuration in three
places: the PKI trust store, the X.509

34
00:02:20.896 --> 00:02:23.930
certificate method, and the authentication

35
00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:27.150
strengths with the policies that use them.

36
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.263
What does Learn leave to you?

37
00:02:32.263 --> 00:02:36.422
The limits are prose, and nobody
measures a CRL until sign-in fails,

38
00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:41.351
so the kit measures bytes and seconds
and compares them with the cloud's limit.

39
00:02:41.351 --> 00:02:44.912
An unreachable endpoint
becomes a finding, not a skip.

40
00:02:44.912 --> 00:02:48.974
A renewed CA with a new thumbprint
would be silently missing,

41
00:02:48.974 --> 00:02:51.737
so each issuing CA is named by thumbprint.

42
00:02:51.737 --> 00:02:54.375
PIV could sign in as single-factor.

43
00:02:54.375 --> 00:02:56.437
An exemption could stay forever.

44
00:02:56.437 --> 00:03:01.450
And a phishing-resistant strength could
exist that no enforced policy requires.

45
00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.690
Each row is pinned by a test.

46
00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.040
The code is one script with two commands.

47
00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:14.260
Measure takes a list of CRL
URLs, downloads each once,

48
00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.990
and writes the bytes, the
seconds and any error.

49
00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:20.898
A failed download is recorded, not raised,

50
00:03:20.898 --> 00:03:24.490
because the unreachable
CRL is itself the finding.

51
00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:27.811
Run it from the internet-facing
path Entra uses,

52
00:03:27.811 --> 00:03:30.365
not from inside the PKI's own network.

53
00:03:30.365 --> 00:03:35.199
Check reads the configuration export,
the measurements and the expected file,

54
00:03:35.199 --> 00:03:37.727
and applies thirteen rules for one cloud.

55
00:03:37.727 --> 00:03:43.350
Five tests cover the chapter, and with the
other chapters' tests, fifty-seven pass.

56
00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.625
Here are the thirteen rules,
each labeled with its source.

57
00:03:50.625 --> 00:03:56.102
Most come straight from Learn: a CA with
no CRL, an endpoint that isn't HTTP,

58
00:03:56.102 --> 00:04:00.001
a CRL over the size limit
or slower than ten seconds,

59
00:04:00.001 --> 00:04:05.850
the certificate method switched off,
and CRL validation off or a CA exempted.

60
00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:10.569
Some are the program's own design:
an early warning at eighty percent,

61
00:04:10.569 --> 00:04:14.361
a CRL nobody measured, a CA
expiring within ninety days,

62
00:04:14.361 --> 00:04:17.563
and an issuing CA missing
from the trust store.

63
00:04:17.563 --> 00:04:21.271
And two protect the strength:
PIV mapped to multifactor,

64
00:04:21.271 --> 00:04:24.530
and an enforced phishing-resistant policy.

65
00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:32.789
The read needs three permissions, each
the least-privileged for its call:

66
00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:37.372
one to read the public key infrastructure,
one to read the authentication

67
00:04:37.372 --> 00:04:41.280
method policy, and one to read
policies, for Conditional Access.

68
00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.659
The code reads the newer
PKI trust store on purpose,

69
00:04:44.659 --> 00:04:50.292
because the older configuration's delegated
read names only Global Administrator.

70
00:04:50.292 --> 00:04:54.885
One judgment call: a root CA with
no CRL is a warning, not a finding,

71
00:04:54.885 --> 00:04:59.655
because a root that issues only
CA certificates may publish none.

72
00:04:59.655 --> 00:05:05.300
Learn's prerequisite is wider, so Lab
2 confirms the root's revocation path.

73
00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:12.375
The verified facts, all
read on November first.

74
00:05:12.375 --> 00:05:16.950
The CRL limits are generally available:
twenty and forty-five megabytes

75
00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:21.525
for interactive sign-in, a ten-second
download, and larger service limits.

76
00:05:21.525 --> 00:05:25.262
Entra checks CRLs up to
ten CAs toward the root.

77
00:05:25.262 --> 00:05:29.918
The trust store, the certificate method,
the strengths and the Conditional Access

78
00:05:29.918 --> 00:05:34.437
policies are all in Graph version one point
oh, with the permissions you just saw.

79
00:05:34.437 --> 00:05:37.230
The certificate method's
bindings include the

80
00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:39.951
low-affinity principal
name and email fields.

81
00:05:39.951 --> 00:05:43.980
And both features are available
in Azure for US Government.

82
00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:51.616
Four places where Learn says
nothing or disagrees with itself,

83
00:05:51.616 --> 00:05:54.530
recorded so a later edit
doesn't loosen the code.

84
00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:59.157
Learn never says which megabyte it means,
so the code takes the stricter one;

85
00:05:59.157 --> 00:06:01.918
Learn also says it plans
to remove the limits.

86
00:06:01.918 --> 00:06:06.118
The federation page still gives
a single twenty-megabyte limit,

87
00:06:06.118 --> 00:06:10.555
so the code follows the CRL page and
applies twenty to GCC either way.

88
00:06:10.555 --> 00:06:15.355
Learn wants a CRL on every CA, but
the check only warns on a root.

89
00:06:15.355 --> 00:06:18.789
And Learn never says which
way binding priority runs,

90
00:06:18.789 --> 00:06:21.800
so the check flags any low-affinity field.

91
00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:27.088
What does this prove federally?

92
00:06:27.088 --> 00:06:32.384
That every issuing CA is trusted with
a CRL Entra can download in time,

93
00:06:32.384 --> 00:06:36.845
which supports acceptance of
PIV credentials and public

94
00:06:36.845 --> 00:06:40.562
key-based authentication,
IA-2 twelve and IA-5 two.

95
00:06:40.562 --> 00:06:45.706
That an enabled policy requires a
phishing-resistant strength and PIV counts as

96
00:06:45.706 --> 00:06:48.778
multifactor, which the
SCuBA baseline requires;

97
00:06:48.778 --> 00:06:51.849
keep those two rows beside
the ScubaGear report.

98
00:06:51.849 --> 00:06:57.650
That a revoked card is refused, which
is FIPS 201's termination of the card.

99
00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:00.890
And that the certificate counts
at the assurance claimed.

100
00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:05.565
Who does what?

101
00:07:05.565 --> 00:07:09.253
Developers build the credential
checks, and need to read Python.

102
00:07:09.253 --> 00:07:14.632
The PKI and credentialing engineers run
the issuing CAs, so they own the list

103
00:07:14.632 --> 00:07:19.927
of CAs by thumbprint, the PIV policy
OIDs, and endpoints that stay available.

104
00:07:19.927 --> 00:07:23.480
And the I S S O keeps
the configuration export,

105
00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:27.128
the findings and every
CRL measurement, dated.

106
00:07:27.128 --> 00:07:30.508
The reader app holds exactly
the three read permissions,

107
00:07:30.508 --> 00:07:32.953
with a certificate or a managed identity.

108
00:07:32.953 --> 00:07:37.630
The code measures and compares; it
never changes the configuration.

109
00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:44.180
Lab 1 runs on a laptop with no tenant.

110
00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:50.167
Step one lists the configuration read: five
calls, under exactly three permissions.

111
00:07:50.167 --> 00:07:52.893
Step two measures the fixture CRLs.

112
00:07:52.893 --> 00:07:56.292
Two are read, with their sizes;
the third file is missing,

113
00:07:56.292 --> 00:07:59.617
and that's recorded with its
error rather than raised.

114
00:07:59.617 --> 00:08:00.930
Your times will differ.

115
00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:03.937
The check in the next step
reads a different file,

116
00:08:03.937 --> 00:08:08.071
the fictional tenant's own
measurements, where one CRL is eighteen

117
00:08:08.071 --> 00:08:12.110
and a half million bytes and
took eleven point four seconds.

118
00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:21.685
Step three checks the fictional tenant for
GCC, with its twenty-million-byte limit.

119
00:08:21.685 --> 00:08:25.660
Five findings and seven warnings,
and the exit code is two.

120
00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:27.635
Read the story in the fixture.

121
00:08:27.635 --> 00:08:33.631
Issuing CA 2 was renewed, so the thumbprint
the card program expects is gone from

122
00:08:33.631 --> 00:08:39.172
the trust store, and the renewed CA's
CRL is large, slow and served over HTTPS.

123
00:08:39.172 --> 00:08:42.148
A legacy intermediate has no CRL at all.

124
00:08:42.148 --> 00:08:46.772
The mode rule maps the wrong
OID, so PIV is single-factor.

125
00:08:46.772 --> 00:08:50.480
And the phishing-resistant
policy is still report-only.

126
00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:57.323
Step four runs the same
tenant for GCC High,

127
00:08:57.323 --> 00:09:00.505
where the limit is
forty-five million bytes.

128
00:09:00.505 --> 00:09:03.030
Now it's five findings and six warnings.

129
00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:06.500
The same eighteen and a half
million bytes is under eighty

130
00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:10.043
percent of the larger limit, so
the early warning drops away.

131
00:09:10.043 --> 00:09:13.993
But the eleven-second download
is a finding in every cloud.

132
00:09:13.993 --> 00:09:16.592
That's why the cloud is
a required argument.

133
00:09:16.592 --> 00:09:19.993
Step five runs the
tests: fifty-seven pass.

134
00:09:19.993 --> 00:09:22.200
Then delete the output folder.

135
00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:30.171
Lab 2 uses a test tenant with
certificate-based authentication

136
00:09:30.171 --> 00:09:33.038
set up for a test CA; the
authors haven't run it.

137
00:09:33.038 --> 00:09:37.438
Register a reader app with a certificate
and only the three permissions.

138
00:09:37.438 --> 00:09:41.237
Export the configuration and check
its shape matches the fixture.

139
00:09:41.237 --> 00:09:44.888
Measure the real CRLs from
outside the agency network.

140
00:09:44.888 --> 00:09:50.076
Run the check with your test PKI's
thumbprints and policy OIDs, and your cloud.

141
00:09:50.076 --> 00:09:55.750
Then the real proof: revoke a test certificate,
wait for the next CRL, and sign in.

142
00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:57.137
Entra should refuse it.

143
00:09:57.137 --> 00:09:59.440
Finally, back everything out.

144
00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:05.502
The dangers, and the guard for each.

145
00:10:05.502 --> 00:10:09.994
A CRL that grows past the limit
takes down every card from that CA,

146
00:10:09.994 --> 00:10:13.639
so the size rule and its
early warning run every time.

147
00:10:13.639 --> 00:10:18.515
A slow or unreachable endpoint causes
the same outage from the network side.

148
00:10:18.515 --> 00:10:22.040
A CA without a CRL lets
revoked cards sign in.

149
00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:25.228
A renewed CA nobody added
locks out new cards.

150
00:10:25.228 --> 00:10:29.177
A troubleshooting exemption that
stays turns revocation off.

151
00:10:29.177 --> 00:10:34.215
PIV mapped as single-factor means
the card isn't what meets MFA.

152
00:10:34.215 --> 00:10:38.240
And a report-only policy is
a requirement only on paper.

153
00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:43.340
Here's what done looks like.

154
00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:47.321
The expected file names every
issuing CA by thumbprint

155
00:10:47.321 --> 00:10:51.215
and every PIV policy OID, with
the limits for your cloud.

156
00:10:51.215 --> 00:10:55.467
Every CRL was measured from outside
the network within the last day,

157
00:10:55.467 --> 00:10:58.353
none near the limit or
slower than ten seconds.

158
00:10:58.353 --> 00:11:03.053
The check shows no finding, and every
exemption has a ticket and an end date.

159
00:11:03.053 --> 00:11:06.653
An enabled policy requires a
phishing-resistant strength.

160
00:11:06.653 --> 00:11:10.327
A revoked test card was refused,
with the evidence filed.

161
00:11:10.327 --> 00:11:14.327
Next, Chapter 6: separation
timing from the logs.

162
00:11:14.327 --> 00:11:15.490
Thanks for watching.
